<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roca–Runciman Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1933</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Roca-Runciman Treaty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Roca–Runciman Treaty was a trade agreement signed in 1933 by Argentine Vice-President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julio-argentino-roca">Julio Argentino Roca</a> Jr and the British trade minister Walter Runciman. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> guaranteed a quota for Argentine chilled beef, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> made concessions to British companies and imports. Widely seen as subservient to British interests, it became a symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/infamous-decade">Infamous Decade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roca-runciman-treaty.jpg" alt="Roca–Runciman Treaty" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roca_Runciman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
